'''Valparaíso''' ([[Baliscano]]: {{W|IPA|[balpaɾaˈiso]}}; {{Literal translation|paradise valley}}; {{Lang-kp|Yvyra-puku}}, {{Literal translation|land of the thunderous forest}}) is the most populous city in and administrative capital of [[Tukupi]], a region of [[Araucaria]]. The city is situated among the foothills of the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] within the [[Alpujarra]] on Balisca's [[Levante, Balisca|Levante]]. Following decades of [[W:densification|densification]] surrounding the [[Castejón Metropolis]], Valparaíso is now considered to be a {{W|satellite city}} of the metropolis, and is included in the [[Concordia|Concordia conurbation]].

Named in reference to the Alpujarra (the surrounding valley), it was founded in 1839 as a {{W|planned community}} for some of the first refugees to be resettled following the conclusion of the [[Baliscan Civil War]]. A vast majority of these families were [[Maroon removal|displaced]] from Southern [[Bayara]], and settled on the [[Mallín Plain]]. A [[W:Open cabildo|special cabildo]] led by [[Marc Yáñez Raya]] resulted in the development of Valparaíso's original grid, with special attention given to it's center.

Amidst the [[Socialist Revolutions|Revolutions of 1917-23]] and liberalizing attitudes related to the {{W|Russian Futurism|futurist movement}} and other cultural movements led to the city becoming one of two (the other being [[Alessandria]]) centers of the early [[Accelerationism|accelerationist]] movement from which the [[Accelerationist International]] emerged, with numerous locals playing major roles in the movement's leadership. 

